#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# null_space

#% text
# `null_space(m)` gives a basis of the vectors `x` with `dot(m, x) == 0`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Exact matrices give an exact basis from the row echelon form; machine matrices give an orthonormal basis from the singular value decomposition.

#% item
# An invertible matrix has the empty null space `[]`.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
null_space([[1, 2], [2, 4]])
#% output
#= [[-2, 1]]

#% cell
null_space([[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6], [7, 8, 9]])
#% output
#= [[1, -2, 1]]

#% cell
null_space([[1, 0], [0, 1]])
#% output
#= []

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# A machine matrix:

#% cell
null_space([[1.0, 1.0], [1.0, 1.0]])
#% output
#= [[0.7071067811865475, -0.7071067811865475]]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The basis vectors are mapped to zero:

#% cell
m = [[1, 1, 1, 1], [1, 2, 3, 4]]; table(dot(m, v), v, null_space(m))
#% output
#= [[0, 0], [0, 0]]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `rank`, `row_reduce`, `linear_solve`
